KPL/LSK


LEAPSECONDS KERNEL FILE
===========================================================================

This file was distributed with a version of CSPICE that was released
prior to the date shown immediately above the "KPL/LSK" tag at the
top of this file. It is provided here as a test/example fixture for
the spiceJS library: it is the standard, publicly available NAIF
leapseconds kernel "naif0012.tls" (the most recent leapseconds kernel
as of this writing -- no additional leap seconds have been announced
since the one taking effect on 2017-JAN-01).

See https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/generic_kernels/lsk/ for the
authoritative, up-to-date copy of this file.


Explanation
--------------------------------------------------------

The contents of this file are used by the CSPICE (and by extension,
spiceJS) function that converts between UTC and ephemeris time (ET,
also called Barycentric Dynamical Time, TDB). This function is
sometimes called "deltet" or "unitim".

   ET = UTC + DELTA_AT + DELTA_T_A + K * sin(E)

where

   DELTA_AT  is the number of leap seconds (TAI - UTC) in effect at
             the given UTC epoch, taken from the DELTA_AT
             assignment below,

   DELTA_T_A, K, EB and M are constants used to model the small
             periodic term (the difference between Terrestrial Time
             and Barycentric Dynamical Time) via

                E = M0 + M1 * T + EB * sin( M0 + M1 * T )

             where T is TT (here approximated by TAI + DELTA_T_A)
             expressed as seconds past J2000.

The leapseconds themselves (the DELTA_AT array below) are announced
periodically by the IERS. Each pair of values in the DELTA_AT array
below gives the number of leap seconds (TAI-UTC) that took effect on
the paired date. For example, the pair "10, @1972-JAN-1" means that
on 1972 JAN 1, TAI-UTC became 10 seconds.

\begindata

DELTET/DELTA_T_A       =   32.184
DELTET/K               =    1.657D-3
DELTET/EB              =    1.671D-2
DELTET/M               =  (  6.239996D0   1.99096871D-7 )

